From: sjm@world.std.com (Sean J Merritt)
Date: Thu, 17 Nov 1994 09:00:06 -0500 (EST)
Subject: [#94-11-247] Wavelets

	I think wavelets are very interesting. I have data from
	an experiment that attempts to screen children for dyslexia
	much earlier than the age where they learn to read.

	The real analysis problem I've had is that data taken with
	an EEG under less than ideal conditions, is riddled with noise.
	I even build a 5'x 3'x 3'faraday cage. It is impractical though
	because typically we take our setup to the children usually
	at schools.

	Traditional Fourier analysis of the data has been marginally
	successful. I may bite the bullet and investigate whether
	wavelets are appropriate for my analysis. We give the subject
	a visual stimulus at low frequency ( a checker board computer
	screen where the black and white squares alternate at the
	driving frequency). I look for a response at twice the driving
	frequency. It's been hit or miss so far.

	Noise is the big concern and I continue to try to improve the
	data taking procedure. If I am succesful and the data is better
	I may have time to "play" a bit with wavelets.

	That intro was brief yet descriptive enough to give me some
	leads. (it's always good to include references).


	-sjm

