On 11/16/94, sullivan@blaze.cs.jhu.edu (Gregory Sullivan) wrote:
>Craig Presson fcp@nuance.com says:
[...]
At least one author did grapple with
>the [AI] concept. Consider the chronological information below.
[...]

>Mechanical devices shaped like animals or humans had begun to pour out of
>the workshops of inventors in the eighteenth century.[...] the German inventor
>Wolfgang von Kempelen built a supposed automaton chess player that defeated
>Napoleon II in a chess competition.  After Von Kempelen's death, a showman
>named Maelzel toured Europe and America with the chess automaton.[...]
> fraud, with a midget inside who moved chess pieces.

Not actually a midget. Several mid-rank chess masters are supposed to
have operated "Maelzel's Automaton"; the largest being about 5' 3".

[...]
>Bierce's incisive mind penetrates to the heart of the issues
>raised [...] Computer scientists, physiologists, psychologists, and
>philosophers are still hotly debating the same questions today.
>
>End of quote from Patricia S. Warrick
>
>The story, in my opinion, is not commensurate with Warrick's hyperbole but
>it does treat the possibility of machine intelligence as a serious
>proposition. [...]

OK, I just reread the story to be sure. I found Warrick's description
hyperbolic all right. The story is a good cautionary tale about safety 
engineering, and that's about all.

Writers of that time period, and for a good while after, were so impressed
by the clockwork automata of the Enlightenment and the early Industrial
Revolution (and some of them *were* amazing) that they did not seriously
confront the difference between an automaton of unspecified but presumably
mechanical or electromechanical design and an AI.

Bierce's story does lead the reader to consider a couple of heady issues:
whether naive reductionism can explain and in fact reproduce purposeful
behavior, and where on the scale of complexity consciousness arises. Full
credit for that, but a D- on development of the premise.

I also looked at Nigel Balchin's "God and the Machine", a 1951 short story
similar to "Moxon's Master" except updated to use a digital computer instead
of clockwork, and with a less violent ending. This story (reprinted in 
_Fantasia Mathematica_) also turns loose more big fish than it catches
(and completely falsifies the nature of programming).




