  Notes From Paglia's Talk at Stanford University, Nov. 9, 1994


On the evening of Nov. 9, 1994 Camille Paglia spoke to a full house
of about 400-500 at Stanford's Kresge Auditorium. It was a Dark and
Stormy Night, but this did not keep the crowd away. What follows
are some random notes I made of some of her more interesting statements. 
This does not purport to be a complete text or summary of what was
said. I tended not to write down those statements that Paglia has already
covered in depth in her writings; to get a perspective of Paglia's
thinking, this summary should be considered just a gloss upon her published 
books (_Sexual Personae_, _Sex, Art, and American Culture_, _Vamps and
Tramps_). The order in which the comments appear below is not
necessarily the order in which they were spoken. Because the order
of Paglia's talk was somewhat random, in some cases I have re-grouped
her comments by subject, if that made them more understandable and
did not change the meaning of them. Some statements are direct quotes, 
and are so indicated; the rest is a summary of what Paglia said, but
not her exact words.

	       Statements by Paglia:

- "Stanford is the P.C. capital of America." Gave some examples
  of censorship by the Politically Correct crowd; how gay activists
  have prevented the screening of her film _Glennda and Camille_
  even though it celebrates gay and lesbian sexuality. "If there
  are Politically Correct people here, you should start tearing
  at your hair, and put on sackcloth and ashes," to atone for your
  wrongdoings.

- "If you had been listening to me, you could have predicted what
  happened yesterday." [Huge election victory for conservative 
  Republicans.] It was "the big Cold Douche for the nation." I 
  predicted this backlash, but you wouldn't listen to me.

- I am a Clinton voter, I support the goals of the Clinton
  administration. But they mismanaged things almost from the
  first day. They are "surrounded by cretins" like Stefanopolis.

- Callous arrogance towards religious belief fuels the right.
  (Gives examples of gay activists insulting religious practices).
  The gay activists are responsible for radicalizing GRANDMOTHERS,
  and for uniting Protestant and Catholic anti-sex activists. For
  400 years those churches have been unable to work together, but 
  this ended when their traditions came under common assault from
  gays and the left.  We must not have the teachers of either the left 
  or the right preaching to kids about sex. My answer is art. It
  always is art. An art-centered curriculum. Art is based around the
  beauty of the human form.

- If the left does not revise its thinking, we will have a
  Republican dynasty. Look at how the Bushes are procreating.
  Progressive thinkers must take responsibility for their
  actions. This arrogance, this demonization of the common
  people must stop. This pushes people to the right. (Mocks
  the morally superior attitude of the left). Elitist behavior
  is not progressive, it is reactionary. That is the message
  of this election - LISTEN to the right. They are doing a far
  better job of communicating with the average person.

- (some hissing is heard) You are SO cowardly, you who hiss!
  This only happens among white bourgeois Americans. Europeans
  don't hiss. Europeans don't even BELIEVE me when I tell them
  that people hiss at the elite schools in America.

- There is every kind of lie being told to young women today,
  by feminists. One of the biggest is how supposedly they're
  discovering all kinds of significant female artists of the
  past who were ignored by the patriarchal power structure.
  Feminists have called attention to some moderately talented 
  women artists, but none of them were really significant. None 
  of them had any lasting impact on other artists. That's what 
  defines a truly great artist - the degree of influence on other 
  artists. The critics don't 'create the reality' of art. They 
  look over the fabric of art, and point out the connections, but 
  they don't create the connections.

- (mockingly) Oh, Dead White Males define the power structure in
  art! Ha. Look at Dante. He was one of the biggest LOSERS in
  history during his lifetime. He spent all his energy writing a
  poem, putting all his enemies in Hell.

- People are saying things like, 'how could a mother like Susan Smith 
  drown her own children? It doesn't seem possible'. "PUH-LEEZ! Euripides
  was writing about this in _Medea_ 400 years B.C." To ask
  such questions only reveals one's ignorance of history and the
  classics. Education has gotten mired down in the present. Every
  great mistake in the present has been made many times before.
  We need to learn from the past.

- Strip clubs are shrines where men go to worship female power.
  I have enormous respect for prostitutes.

- "I don't consider myself a woman." I'm some weird, ambiguous
  thing.

- My aim is to close down these departments of "Womens Studies"
  and "Gay Studies." They promote such terrible scholarship. There 
  are no academic qualifications to join such departments anyway, 
  just 'I'm a woman' or 'I'm gay.' I would replace them with "Sex
  Studies," and study the entire spectrum of human sexuality.
  And not "Gender Studies" either, because "gender" is really
  this idea that sex roles are arbitrary social constructs,
  and of course they're not.

- Sexuality is a continuum. There is no such thing as completely
  gay or straight. I despise anything that sets gays apart.
  I want to integrate their experience into the mainstream.

- "I'm running on a platform of pro-penis lesbianism."

- I view the human situation as a comedy, not as a tragedy.

- (The first question attempts to paint Paglia's viewpoint as
  being somehow harmful to "people of color.") Paglia: what
  P.C. language is in that question! It divides up the world
  neatly into those who are "for" people of color, and those
  who are "against" them. That's absurd. It's like, 'since I
  am 'for' people of color, anyone who disagrees with me must
  be 'against' them.' That kind of language is now 25 years 
  old. It has the stench of a corpse.

- (Question: an incident is recounted in which a gay man in San
  Francisco was shot while walking down the street with his
  gay lover. What is to be done about such oppression?) Paglia:
  I don't know the circumstances of that particular incident.
  In any case, the "anecdote of oppression" is a form
  of "sentimentality". The facts of the case
  may not be as the news media report it. That may have been
  his ex-lover who shot him. We're not certain it's homophobia.
  And even if it is, the street is "pagan", it is "hostile
  territory for everyone." Gay men are constantly seeking to
  press the limits of sexual expression. Their disasters are
  a consequence of their quest for sexual adventure. 

- Most situations involving 'battered women' are complex games or
  dramas being played out by both parties. Most 'battered
  women' taunt their batterers, finding all his hot buttons and
  pushing them. They fight, then reconcile, and have their best sex 
  ever during the reconciliation; they then become "addicted to the
  forgiveness." There are white, middle-class women who have a taste 
  for S&M relationships; it's not only gay men. Nicole Simpson called
  the police on her husband somewhere from 8 to 30 times, but each
  time she refused to press charges. So clearly she was playing
  out some sort of little game. This does not justify her murder,
  and if O.J. is guilty, he should be severely punished. However,
  it does mean that she was not a pure, innocent victim, but
  a worldly woman playing a dangerous game of sex, money, and power.

- Anyone who stays in a battering relationship after the first
  incident of battering is a co-dependent. I ended a lesbian
  relationship of 2 years on the day that my partner roughed
  me up. She begged forgiveness, and said that it would never
  happen again. I said, you did it once, you'll do it again,
  and I walked out.

- I can always tell a liberal on the street. They have large,
  ill-behaved dogs, that they refuse to discipline. They don't
  know how to tell their dog "NO!".

- (Question: Two years at the Stanford Graduate school has almost
  convinced me that I don't want a PhD. So much pressure to conform
  to Political Correctness. What should I do?) Paglia: Stay and
  fight! I hear this same question all the time coming from the
  elite schools. The most creative people of my generation, the
  1960s radicals, did not stay in graduate schools. Those who did
  had their careers hurt by their nonconformity. Just look at
  what happened to me! You must learn to talk to the media, to
  go outside the usual channels. You must help bridge the gap
  between academia and the media. People are losing contact with
  the classics of civilization, with the past. To save our
  language and literature, you must stay in the university, 
  and re-vivify it.

	     (end of statements by Paglia)

Somehow I did not previously realize how short Paglia is. Somebody 
said she is 4' 11'' tall. Her head could barely be seen over the
speaker's platform. 

When she began speaking, the sheer speed of her delivery seemed
astonishing, and impossible to maintain. But maintain it she did.
Her level of energy was incredible. She spoke without notes for
almost an hour, then answered questions submitted on cards for about 
another hour.  Only a few times did she pause to take a sip of 
water.  Each question was answered with a mini-sermon of about ten 
minutes. After that, she signed books in the lobby for at least
another hour, which again involved almost nonstop talking.
Having given talks myself, I know how exhausting this can be,
even though my words-per-minute rate is no more than half what
Paglia's is. This woman has a metabolic disorder. Her body must
be transforming glucose into methamphetamines.

While there was some hissing during her talk, and some of the 
questions were hostile, on the whole the crowd seemed quite 
appreciative and supportive of Paglia.

